  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to flatter; to butter up",
      "explanation": "To praise someone excessively, often insincerely, to put them in a good mood or get them to do something."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "17564_odateru_ex1",
      "japanese": "{部下|ぶか}をおだてて{仕事|しごと}をさせる。",
      "english": "To flatter subordinates into doing work.",
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "17564_odateru_ex2",
      "japanese": "おだてられると{調子|ちょうし}に{乗|の}ってしまう。",
      "english": "When flattered, I tend to get carried away.",
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "17564_odateru_ex3",
      "japanese": "{子供|こども}をおだてて{勉強|べんきょう}させようとしたが、{見抜|みぬ}かれた。",
      "english": "I tried to coax the child into studying by flattering them, but they saw through it.",
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY:\nTransitive verb ({他動詞|たどうし}). The person being flattered takes を.\n\nPROVERB:\n- {豚|ぶた}もおだてりゃ{木|き}に{登|のぼ}る - Even a pig will climb a tree if you flatter it enough (flattery can motivate anyone)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- おだてる - to flatter (with insincere praise to get something)\n- {褒|ほ}める - to praise (can be genuine)\n- お{世辞|せじ}を{言|い}う - to pay compliments (formal flattery)\n- {持|も}ち{上|あ}げる - to praise/elevate someone\n\nUSAGE:\nOften implies the flattery is insincere or has an ulterior motive.",
